A student writes this method to count how many elements in an array of integers are even:

public static int countEvens(int[] arr) {
    int count = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
        if (arr[i] % 2 = 0) {
            count++;
        }
    }
    return count;
}

Assuming the single syntax error is fixed, what does this method correctly compute, and why is count initialized to 0?

Consider the following method intended to reverse the contents of array arr in place:

public static void reverseArray(int[] arr) {
    for (int i = 0; i < arr.length / 2; i++) {
        int temp = arr[i];
        arr[i] = arr[arr.length - 1 - i];
        arr[arr.length - 1 - i] = temp;
    }
}

Why does the loop use i < arr.length / 2 instead of i < arr.length?
